    cons: [
      "Not thick enough to level any meaningful subfloor irregularity",
      "Foam offers less acoustic performance than cork or rubber",
      "Vapour barrier only works if the seams are actually taped",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The sensible default beneath rigid core vinyl that has no attached pad. Thin enough to be safe, with the moisture barrier built in.",
    description:
      "Most vinyl plank underlayment failures come from using something too soft, so the first virtue of a product in this category is restraint. FloorLot Blue is 2mm of firm closed-cell foam, which is deliberately thinner than what people instinctively reach for, and that is exactly why it works. In our joint-stress testing, loading rigid core planks repeatedly over each pad, the thin firm foams showed no measurable joint movement while thicker soft pads let the planks flex enough to work the locking edges. The integrated vapour barrier is the second reason to choose it. Over a concrete slab, moisture rising from below will condense under a floating floor and grow mould in the void regardless of the planks being waterproof, and a barrier is the only thing that prevents it. Here the film is bonded to the foam with an adhesive overlap strip, so sealing the seams is a matter of peeling a liner rather than running separate tape. Acoustic performance is decent rather than exceptional. It takes the sharp edge off footfall and improves the hollow sound of rigid core over plywood, but it does not match cork or rubber. It also cannot level a subfloor — at 2mm nothing can, and expecting an underlayment to fix flatness is the second most common mistake in this category.",
  },
  {
    id: "quietwalk-lv",
    name: "QuietWalk LV",
    category: "Recycled Fibre, 1.5mm, Moisture Barrier",
    badge: "Best Sound Reduction",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.6,
    reviewCount: 6200,
    pros: [
      "Engineered specifically for luxury vinyl, so the density suits rigid core joints",
      "Best impact sound reduction of any thin underlayment we tested",
      "Recycled fibre construction with genuine acoustic performance rather than just cushioning",
      "Moisture barrier film bonded to the underside",
      "Fibre structure allows lateral moisture dispersion rather than trapping it in one spot",
    ],
    cons: [
      "More expensive than basic foam per square foot",
      "Fibre compresses slightly over years under heavy point loads",
      "Still cannot compensate for an uneven subfloor",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The one to buy for an upstairs room or an apartment. Purpose-built for vinyl and genuinely quieter than foam.",
    description:
      "Most underlayment marketed for vinyl is generic foam relabelled, and the difference shows in an upstairs installation where footfall transmits to the room below. QuietWalk LV is engineered for the application, using a dense recycled fibre mat rather than closed-cell foam, and in our impact sound testing it was clearly the best performer among the thin products that are safe under rigid core. The reason is structural. Fibre absorbs and dissipates impact energy across the mat rather than simply compressing under it, which is why it reduces the transmitted thud without adding the softness that causes joint failure. At 1.5mm it is thin enough that rigid planks stay properly supported, and in our joint-stress testing it showed none of the flex that thicker cushioning pads allowed. The bonded moisture barrier is appropriate for slab installations, and the fibre structure has a useful secondary property: it lets small amounts of moisture disperse laterally rather than pooling in one place beneath the planks. The costs are price and long-term compression. It is meaningfully more expensive per square foot than basic foam, and under sustained heavy point loads the fibre does compress slightly over years. Neither undermines it for the job it is bought for, which is quieting a floor above a living space.",
  },
  {
    id: "mp-global-insulayment",
    name: "MP Global Insulayment",
    category: "Recycled Fibre, 1.5mm, Thermal + Acoustic",
    badge: "Best for Concrete Slabs",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 3100,
    pros: [
      "Genuine thermal resistance, which noticeably warms a floor over a cold slab",
      "Good acoustic performance from a dense fibre construction",
      "Thin enough to be safe under rigid core locking systems",
      "Made from recycled material with low environmental impact",
      "Lies exceptionally flat with no memory curl from the roll",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Requires a separate vapour barrier over concrete, as it has none built in",
      "Costs more than plain foam",
      "Thermal benefit is modest compared with a properly insulated slab",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The pick for a cold basement slab. It takes the chill out of the floor in a way plain foam does not.",
    description:
      "Vinyl over a concrete slab is cold, and rigid SPC is the coldest construction in the category because dense stone-plastic conducts heat away from your feet efficiently. Insulayment addresses that specifically, using a dense recycled fibre mat with genuine thermal resistance rather than the negligible R-value of thin foam. In our slab testing the surface temperature difference was small in absolute terms and clearly noticeable barefoot, which is what actually matters in a basement family room. Acoustically it performs well for its thickness, damping the footfall sharpness that rigid core produces over hard substrate, and at 1.5mm it stays within the range that keeps locking joints properly supported. It also lies remarkably flat straight off the roll, with none of the edge curl that makes some underlayments awkward to work with single-handed. The important caveat is moisture. This product has no vapour barrier built in, and over concrete that is not optional — slabs release moisture continuously and it will condense under a floating floor. You need to lay six mil polyethylene first and tape the seams, which adds a step and a material cost that the all-in-one products avoid. Budget for both rather than discovering the omission after the underlayment is down.",
  },
  {
    id: "roberts-super-felt",
    name: "Roberts Super Felt",
    category: "Felt, 3mm, Premium Acoustic",
    badge: "Best Premium Acoustic",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.6,
    reviewCount: 4400,
    pros: [
      "Excellent sound absorption, among the best available in any underlayment",
      "Dense felt construction that supports planks rather than cushioning them",
      "Very good thermal performance over cold subfloors",
      "Durable enough that it will outlast the flooring above it",
      "Available with or without an attached vapour barrier",
    ],
    cons: [
      "At 3mm it is at the upper limit of what rigid core vinyl tolerates",
      "Heavier and stiffer to handle and cut than foam",
      "Considerably more expensive than basic underlayment",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The best acoustic result you can get under vinyl, with one warning: check your flooring warranty allows 3mm before buying it.",
    description:
      "Felt is what serious acoustic underlayment is made of, and Roberts Super Felt is the reason. Dense compressed felt absorbs impact energy exceptionally well while remaining firm under load, which is a combination foam cannot achieve — foam that absorbs sound well is soft enough to let planks flex. In our testing it produced the largest reduction in transmitted footfall of any product here, meaningfully better than the thin fibre mats. It performs well thermally too, which makes it attractive over a cold slab or an unheated crawl space. The felt is durable enough that it will comfortably outlast the flooring laid on top, and it is available in versions with a vapour barrier bonded on for slab installations. The critical caveat is thickness. At 3mm it sits at the top of what rigid core vinyl manufacturers permit, and a number of warranties specify a maximum underlayment thickness below that. Exceeding the specified thickness is the classic route to failed locking joints, so this is a product to buy only after reading your specific flooring warranty rather than assuming thicker is better. It is also stiffer to handle and cut than foam, and considerably more expensive, so it is worth the money specifically when acoustic isolation is the goal.",
  },
