    bottomLine:
      "Excellent acoustically and naturally mould-resistant. Just remember it needs its own vapour barrier over concrete.",
    description:
      "Cork has been used under flooring for a very long time because it does several things at once that synthetic materials struggle to combine. It is dense enough to support a rigid plank firmly while still absorbing impact energy, it insulates genuinely well, and it is naturally antimicrobial, which is a meaningful property in a basement where any organic material under a floor is a mould risk. In our testing it performed close to felt acoustically while feeling firmer underfoot. At 3mm it sits at the upper end of what rigid core vinyl warranties permit, so checking the specific flooring documentation matters before buying. The firmness is what makes it acceptable at that thickness where a soft 3mm foam would not be: cork compresses very little under load, so the planks stay supported and the locking joints do not work. The limitation to plan around is moisture. Cork itself resists mould but will degrade if kept wet, and it does not block vapour, so a concrete slab installation needs six mil polyethylene laid and taped underneath it. That is an extra step and cost. On a plywood subfloor above grade, none of that applies and cork is an excellent, environmentally sensible choice.",
