    rating: 4.8,
    reviewCount: 1,
    pros: [
      "Costs nothing extra and removes an entire installation step",
      "Guaranteed compatible with the flooring warranty by definition",
      "No risk of exceeding the maximum thickness the locking system tolerates",
      "Manufacturer has matched the pad density to their own joint design",
      "Eliminates the most common cause of failed vinyl plank installations",
    ],
    cons: [
      "You cannot upgrade to better acoustic performance without removing the pad",
      "Still requires a separate vapour barrier over concrete on most products",
      "Attached pads are generally thin, so sound reduction is modest",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "If your planks have a pad bonded on, adding another underlayment is a mistake. This entry exists because it is the most common error we see.",
    description:
      "This is not a product to buy, and it is here because the single most frequent underlayment question has an answer people do not expect: do nothing. A large proportion of rigid core vinyl now ships with an IXPE foam or cork pad already bonded to the underside, and adding a second underlayment beneath that is not an upgrade. It is the classic route to a failed floor. The mechanism is straightforward. Locking systems on rigid core planks are engineered around a specific amount of support beneath them. Stack two compressible layers and the planks flex further under foot traffic than the joint was designed to tolerate, which works the locking edges until they separate, crack, or develop gaps that will not close. In our joint-stress testing, doubled pads produced measurable movement within a fraction of the cycles the single-pad samples withstood. Manufacturers know this, which is why most warranties explicitly void if an additional underlayment is used beneath an attached pad. The one exception is a vapour barrier over concrete, which is a thin film rather than a cushion and adds no compressibility, so it belongs under everything. Check the underside of a plank before buying anything else in this guide.",
  },
];

