            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Two millimetres is a sensible default and three is typically the maximum any manufacturer permits. Firmness matters more than thickness: a dense 3mm cork can be safer than a soft 2mm foam, because compression under load is what stresses the joints. Always check the specific flooring warranty rather than the underlayment packaging.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do I need a vapour barrier over concrete?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes, always, regardless of the planks being waterproof. Slabs release moisture upward continuously, and because vinyl blocks it completely, that vapour condenses in the void beneath a floating floor and grows mould unseen. Use six mil polyethylene or an underlayment with a bonded barrier, and tape the seams.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can underlayment level an uneven subfloor?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "No. At 2 to 3mm nothing can, and expecting it to is a common misconception. Rigid planks bridge dips rather than following them, so an uneven subfloor produces rocking, creaking, and eventually cracked joints. Manufacturers typically require flatness within about 3/16 inch over 10 feet, achieved with levelling compound or sanding.",
          },
        },
