        <!-- SECTION 1: Do You Actually Need Underlayment? -->
        <div id="do-you-need-one" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Do You Actually Need Underlayment?
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the first question and it has three possible answers depending on what you bought and what you are laying it on. Getting it wrong is more consequential here than in almost any other flooring decision.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">If Your Planks Have an Attached Pad</h3>

          <p>
            Do not add a separate underlayment. A large share of rigid core vinyl now ships with IXPE foam or cork bonded to the underside, and stacking another compressible layer beneath it lets the planks flex more than the locking joints tolerate. Most warranties void explicitly for this. Over concrete you still need a vapour barrier, but that is a thin film rather than a cushion.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">If Your Planks Have No Attached Pad</h3>

          <p>
            You want a thin, firm underlayment. It smooths minor subfloor texture, cushions slightly, reduces the hollow sound rigid core makes over plywood, and over concrete carries the vapour barrier. Two millimetres is a sensible default and three is generally the maximum any manufacturer permits.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Over Concrete, Moisture Handling Is Mandatory Either Way</h3>

          <p>
            Slabs release moisture upward continuously. Vinyl planks do not care, but that vapour condenses in the void beneath a floating floor, and mould grows there unseen. Whether it comes as a bonded film on an underlayment or a separate sheet of six mil polyethylene, a barrier is not optional over a slab.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">For Glue-Down Vinyl, Use Nothing</h3>

          <p>
            Glue-down installations bond directly to the subfloor. Underlayment is not just unnecessary, it prevents the adhesive from doing its job. If you are gluing, the subfloor preparation replaces the underlayment entirely and needs to be correspondingly better.
          </p>

