        <!-- SECTION 2: Why Thicker Underlayment Breaks Vinyl Floors -->
        <div id="thickness-trap" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Why Thicker Underlayment Breaks Vinyl Floors
          </h2>

          <p>
            Intuition says a thicker, softer pad produces a more comfortable and quieter floor. With rigid core vinyl that intuition is actively wrong, and it causes more failed installations than any other single decision.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Rigid planks do not bend, they lever.</strong> A rigid core plank spans across whatever is under it. Where the support is soft, foot traffic pushes the plank down and the movement concentrates at the locking joint on each edge, working it like a hinge with every step.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Locking joints have a fatigue limit.</strong> The interlocking profile is precisely machined plastic and stone composite. It tolerates a defined amount of flex. Beyond that, edges gradually deform, then gap, then chip, and eventually the joint releases entirely. Once a joint has failed the plank cannot be re-locked in place.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>The failure appears months later.</strong> This is what makes it so common. The floor looks perfect on day one and develops gaps, clicking, or visible movement after a season of use, by which point the underlayment is buried and the cause is invisible.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Firmness matters more than thickness.</strong> A dense 3mm felt or cork can be safer than a soft 2mm foam, because compression under load is what matters rather than the number on the packaging. Where a manufacturer specifies a maximum, it is generally assuming a firm product.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Check the flooring warranty, not the underlayment marketing.</strong> Underlayment packaging frequently advertises comfort and thickness because those sell. The binding document is your flooring warranty, which will state a maximum thickness and often a required density. That is the specification to buy against.
          </p>
        </div>

