        <!-- SECTION 4: Vapour Barriers and Concrete Slabs -->
        <div id="moisture" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Vapour Barriers and Concrete Slabs
          </h2>

          <p>
            Over a concrete slab, moisture management is the most important function underlayment performs, and it is the one people most often skip because vinyl is waterproof.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Concrete never stops releasing moisture.</strong> Even a slab poured decades ago wicks ground moisture upward continuously. In an open room it evaporates unnoticed. Under a floating floor it reaches the cold underside of the planks, condenses, and has nowhere to go.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Waterproof planks make it worse, not better.</strong> Because the vinyl blocks the vapour completely, moisture accumulates in the void rather than passing through and evaporating. Mould grows on the underlayment, on the subfloor, and in the joints, entirely out of sight until there is a smell.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Six mil polyethylene is the standard.</strong> Either bonded to the underlayment or laid as a separate sheet, with seams overlapped by several inches and taped, and the film turned up the wall behind the trim. Gaps in the barrier defeat the purpose, so the taping matters as much as the film.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Test the slab before you start.</strong> Tape a two-foot square of plastic sheet tightly to the concrete and leave it for a couple of days. Condensation underneath, or a darkened patch of slab, means active moisture. Heavy moisture may need a sealer or a professional assessment rather than just a film.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Above-grade plywood does not need one.</strong> A wood subfloor over a ventilated space is not a moisture source in the way a slab is, and a barrier there can trap moisture from the wrong side. Use a barrier over concrete and skip it over plywood unless the manufacturer says otherwise.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">The Test That Takes Two Days</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              Before laying anything over concrete, tape a square of clear plastic tightly to the slab and leave it 48 hours. Moisture beading underneath means the slab is actively releasing vapour, and you need a proper barrier and possibly a slab sealer. This costs nothing, takes two minutes to set up, and prevents the single most expensive failure in basement flooring.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5: Getting the Sound Right -->
        <div id="sound" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Getting the Sound Right
          </h2>

          <p>
            Rigid core vinyl over a hard subfloor produces a sharp, hollow report underfoot, and underlayment is the main lever available for improving it. Two different measurements matter and they are frequently confused.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Impact sound is what you hear downstairs.</strong> Footsteps, dropped objects, and chair legs transmit through the floor into the room below. This is measured as IIC, and it is the number that matters in an upstairs installation or an apartment. Dense fibre, felt, and cork perform considerably better here than foam.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Airborne sound is the hollowness you hear in the room.</strong> That sharp click of rigid planks over plywood is the floor resonating in the void beneath. A firmer, denser pad damps that resonance, which is why felt and cork make a floor sound solid where thin foam only makes it quieter.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Apartments usually have a required rating.</strong> Many condo and co-op boards specify a minimum IIC for hard flooring, and installing below it can mean being required to remove the floor. Check the building rules before choosing, because compliant underlayment is often thicker and needs to be reconciled with your flooring warranty.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Softer is not automatically quieter.</strong> Beyond a point, a soft pad stops improving sound and starts allowing plank flex, which produces its own creaking and clicking as joints move. The best acoustic underlayments are dense rather than squashy.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>The room matters as much as the floor.</strong> Hard walls, minimal furnishing, and no rugs make any hard floor sound sharp regardless of what is beneath it. If sound is the goal, an area rug does more for the room you are standing in than any underlayment does.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Laying It Correctly -->
        <div id="installation" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Laying It Correctly
          </h2>

          <p>
            Underlayment is straightforward to install and the errors are all small, repeatable, and easy to avoid once you know them.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Butt the seams, never overlap the foam.</strong> Overlapping creates a ridge that the planks bridge, producing a raised line across the floor and a flex point at the joints. Lay the rolls edge to edge and tape the seam. The exception is the vapour barrier film, which is designed to overlap.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Run it perpendicular to the planks.</strong> Laying underlayment across the direction of the flooring means plank seams and underlayment seams never coincide, which avoids concentrating a soft spot exactly where a joint sits.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Do not cover the whole floor at once.</strong> Unroll and lay only a few rows ahead of the flooring. Underlayment left exposed gets walked on, dragged over, torn by tools, and shifted out of position, and a torn barrier over concrete is worse than none because it channels moisture to one spot.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Turn the barrier up the wall.</strong> Over concrete, run the film a couple of inches up the perimeter wall so slab moisture cannot come around the edge. Trim it flush after the trim goes on. This detail is skipped constantly and it is where basement floors fail first.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Keep the expansion gap clear.</strong> The underlayment should not be packed tightly into the gap at the walls, because it can transmit pressure back into the floating floor when the planks expand. Trim it to the wall rather than stuffing it in.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Sweep the subfloor first, properly.</strong> A single screw head, a lump of dried adhesive, or a stone under the underlayment becomes a permanent high spot that the plank levers over, and it will eventually crack a joint. Vacuum rather than brush, and run a hand over the surface to feel for anything a brush missed.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 7: Foam, Fibre, Cork and Rubber Compared -->
        <div id="materials" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Foam, Fibre, Cork and Rubber Compared
          </h2>

          <p>
            Underlayment is sold by material as much as by thickness, and the four families available behave differently enough that the choice is worth making deliberately rather than taking whatever the flooring aisle stocks.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Closed-cell foam is the default and the cheapest.</strong> Polyethylene foam rolls out fast, usually comes with a vapour barrier bonded to one face, and does an adequate job of everything without excelling at anything. It is the right choice when you need a barrier over concrete and a modest amount of cushioning, and it is what most 3-in-1 products use.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Recycled fibre is the acoustic upgrade.</strong> Compressed fibre pads are denser than foam at the same thickness, which is exactly the combination rigid vinyl wants: real sound damping without the softness that works the locking joints. They also allow a small amount of airflow beneath the floor, which helps a slab breathe where a sealed foam does not.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Cork is the natural option and it is genuinely good.</strong> It damps impact sound well, insulates against a cold slab, resists mould, and holds its thickness over years rather than compressing permanently the way cheap foam does. It costs more, and it needs a separate vapour barrier over concrete because cork itself will absorb moisture.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Rubber is excellent and usually the wrong choice here.</strong> Rubber underlayment outperforms everything for sound and is the standard in commercial and multi-family construction. Under residential vinyl it is expensive, heavy to handle, and frequently thicker than a flooring warranty permits. It also stains some vinyl backings, so check compatibility before committing.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Match the material to the failure you are trying to prevent.</strong> Cold slab means fibre or cork. Footsteps carrying to the room below means fibre or cork again. Slab moisture means foam with a bonded barrier, or any pad over a separate film. Nothing to prevent, and planks with a pad already attached, means buying nothing at all.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 8: Frequently Asked Questions -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best underlayment for vinyl plank flooring?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For rigid core vinyl without an attached pad, FloorLot Blue 3-in-1 is the sensible default at 2mm with a built-in vapour barrier. QuietWalk LV is the best choice for an upstairs room where sound transmission matters, and MP Global Insulayment adds genuine thermal benefit over a cold concrete slab.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need underlayment if my vinyl plank has an attached pad?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No, and adding one is a common cause of failed floors. Stacking a second compressible layer lets rigid planks flex more than the locking joints tolerate, which works the edges until they gap or crack. Most warranties void explicitly for this. Over concrete you still need a vapour barrier, but that is a thin film, not a cushion.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How thick should vinyl plank underlayment be?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Two millimetres is a sensible default and three is typically the maximum any manufacturer permits. Firmness matters more than thickness: a dense 3mm cork can be safer than a soft 2mm foam, because compression under load is what stresses the joints. Always check the specific flooring warranty rather than the underlayment packaging.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a vapour barrier over concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, always, regardless of the planks being waterproof. Slabs release moisture upward continuously, and because vinyl blocks it completely, that vapour condenses in the void beneath a floating floor and grows mould unseen. Use six mil polyethylene or an underlayment with a bonded barrier, and tape the seams.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can underlayment level an uneven subfloor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. At 2 to 3mm nothing can, and expecting it to is a common misconception. Rigid planks bridge dips rather than following them, so an uneven subfloor produces rocking, creaking, and eventually cracked joints. Manufacturers typically require flatness within about 3/16 inch over 10 feet, achieved with levelling compound or sanding.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I use underlayment with glue-down vinyl?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Glue-down installations bond the plank directly to the subfloor, and underlayment prevents the adhesive from doing its job. For glue-down, the subfloor preparation replaces the underlayment entirely, which means the surface needs to be cleaner, flatter, and better prepared than a floating installation requires.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Underlayment Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Underlayment errors are unusual in that they rarely show up immediately. The floor looks perfect, and the consequences arrive months later.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Adding Underlayment Over an Attached Pad</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                This is the most common vinyl plank installation failure and it is entirely avoidable. Doubling the cushion lets rigid planks flex beyond what the locking joints tolerate, producing gaps, clicking, and separated seams after a season. Check the underside of a plank before buying anything, and if there is foam or cork bonded on, buy only a vapour barrier if you need one.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing Thicker for Comfort</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Softness under a rigid plank is not comfort, it is leverage on the joint. Underlayment marketing sells thickness because it sounds better, while the flooring warranty specifies a maximum because the engineering demands it. When the two conflict, the warranty is the document that matters.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Vapour Barrier Over Concrete</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Waterproof planks make slab moisture worse rather than better, because the vapour cannot pass through and instead condenses in the void underneath. Mould grows there invisibly until it smells. Six mil polyethylene with taped seams, turned up the wall at the perimeter, prevents it entirely for very little cost.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Overlapping the Foam at the Seams</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Overlapping creates a ridge that planks bridge across, producing a visible line and a permanent flex point exactly where the underlayment doubles. Butt the foam edge to edge and tape the joint. Only the vapour barrier film is designed to overlap, and that overlap belongs beneath the pad.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

